1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,400
[Night. Lamplight. A hand in a brown wool cuff taps a Morse key: one… two-two… three-three-three.]

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:05,530
[A sunlit kitchen. Kai yawns over a mug. Mira draws at the table. Biscuit sits very politely beside the toast.]

3
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
NARRATOR: I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

4
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,290
[Close on Mira finishing a crayon sun. She holds it up, proud.]

5
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,570
NARRATOR: I was drawing the Sun.

6
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:11,095
[Kai, sleepy, squints at her over his mug.]

7
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:10,875
NARRATOR: Who draws at six in the morning?

8
00:00:11,245 --> 00:00:13,095
[Mira, without looking up.]

9
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:12,505
MIRA: Artists.

10
00:00:13,245 --> 00:00:15,950
[Close on Biscuit. His eyes slide slowly toward the toast on the edge of the table.]

11
00:00:13,345 --> 00:00:15,730
NARRATOR: And Biscuit was planning a crime.

12
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:19,250
[Sydney, mid-afternoon. A surfer stands up on the wave of her life.]

13
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,385
NARRATOR: At that moment, half the world was in sunlight.

14
00:00:19,755 --> 00:00:21,605
[A paddy field before noon. A farmer straightens up and squints at the sky.]

15
00:00:21,755 --> 00:00:24,605
[A boy sprints for his school bus. The doors close on his nose and it pulls away without him.]

16
00:00:24,755 --> 00:00:26,605
[Back in the kitchen, Biscuit rises on his back legs and snatches the toast. Kai lunges.]

17
00:00:24,855 --> 00:00:26,245
NARRATOR: Biscuit, no!

18
00:00:26,755 --> 00:00:29,410
[Mid-snatch, the sunlight snaps off. Everyone freezes in the blue dark. Biscuit still has the toast.]

19
00:00:26,855 --> 00:00:28,590
NARRATOR: Then the Sun went out.

20
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:30,510
[Sydney: the same wave, the same surfer, under sudden stars.]

21
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:31,610
[Tamil Nadu: the paddy goes black at noon.]

22
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:32,710
[Manila: the boy at the empty bus stop, in the dark.]

23
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:34,920
[Through the kitchen window: stars over the city at breakfast time.]

24
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,400
MIRA: Is it night again?

25
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:37,675
[Kai, staring at the window.]

26
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:37,055
NARRATOR: It's seven in the morning.

27
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:40,755
[Three hard knocks rattle the front door.]

28
00:00:37,925 --> 00:00:40,535
NARRATOR: Then someone knocked. Three times.

29
00:00:40,905 --> 00:00:44,055
[The door opens on Mr. Ito from upstairs: fully dressed at dawn, shoes on, torch, a radio, a packed canvas bag.]

30
00:00:41,005 --> 00:00:44,920
MR. ITO: Good morning. Shoes on, please. Both of you.

31
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:47,970
[Close on Ito, calm in the torchlight.]

32
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:47,750
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito, from upstairs.

33
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,270
[Close on the canvas bag in his hand.]

34
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:52,635
NARRATOR: He had a torch, a radio, and a bag he'd packed the night before.

35
00:00:53,005 --> 00:00:56,155
[Ito points past them at Biscuit, who still has the toast.]

36
00:00:53,105 --> 00:00:56,020
MR. ITO: And bring the dog. And the toast.

37
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:59,540
[Title: THE SUN WENT OUT.]

38
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:58,980
NARRATOR: It took me a year to understand why.

39
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:03,230
[Outside, the street is full. Pyjamas and work clothes, every phone held up to a starry sky.]

40
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:03,010
NARRATOR: Outside, the whole street was looking up.

41
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:05,230
[A man in a dressing gown shouts at the sky.]

42
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:04,790
NARRATOR: It's an eclipse!

43
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:08,530
[Ito, unimpressed, to Mira.]

44
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:09,770
MR. ITO: Eclipses are polite. They leave after seven minutes.

45
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,875
[Ito points up at the roof of their building.]

46
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:12,655
MR. ITO: Roof. Bring the telescope.

47
00:01:13,025 --> 00:01:16,175
[The stairwell, sixth floor. Ito climbs ahead with the torch. Kai hauls the heavy telescope case up, puffing. Mira skips behind.]

48
00:01:13,125 --> 00:01:17,310
NARRATOR: Kai carried it up six floors. He complained about every one.

49
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:19,765
[The roof. Ito sets up a brass telescope with quick, sure hands.]

50
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,545
MR. ITO: Venus first.

51
00:01:19,915 --> 00:01:22,050
[Low in the east, one bright point still shining: Venus.]

52
00:01:20,015 --> 00:01:21,830
MIRA: Why is that one still on?

53
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,350
[Diagram: the Sun as a dark question mark, Venus catching its light and passing it on to Earth.]

54
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,490
MR. ITO: Venus shines with the Sun's light.

55
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:29,010
[The light path traced: Sun to Venus, Venus to Earth. The long way round.]

56
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:29,775
MR. ITO: That light takes the long way round to reach us.

57
00:01:30,145 --> 00:01:33,295
[Ito, lit by the torch, eyes on his watch.]

58
00:01:30,245 --> 00:01:35,810
MR. ITO: If something happened at the Sun, Venus goes dark at four minutes past seven.

59
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,890
[They sit on the roof and wait. Biscuit finally finishes the toast. Kai checks his watch: 7:03.]

60
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:37,670
NARRATOR: So we waited.

61
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,290
[At four minutes past seven, Venus flickers and goes out.]

62
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,590
[Ito, very quietly.]

63
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:44,130
MR. ITO: Then it happened at the Sun.

64
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:48,050
[Diagram: Jupiter, far out. A long light path. A clock.]

65
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,165
MR. ITO: And Jupiter will go out at four minutes past eight.

66
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:52,685
[Jupiter, centred in the telescope crosshairs, winks out.]

67
00:01:49,635 --> 00:01:52,100
NARRATOR: At four minutes past eight, it did.

68
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:56,020
[Ito taps the telescope three times, humming under his breath.]

69
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:56,610
NARRATOR: He always tapped it three times. I never asked why.

70
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:59,830
[A small dome on a dark mountain. Beside it a radio dish turns toward one bright star.]

71
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:03,030
[A small observatory at night. A young man with round glasses bends over a light table.]

72
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:06,230
[An old eclipse plate under a magnifier. One faint line through the corona, far too straight. A red pen circles it.]

73
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:09,215
[Close on Kai’s watch: the thermometer ticks down a degree.]

74
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,995
NARRATOR: By nine, it was a degree colder.

75
00:02:09,365 --> 00:02:12,515
[Ito’s battery radio on the kitchen table.]

76
00:02:09,465 --> 00:02:13,805
(RADIO): Phone networks are down across the city. Stay indoors.

77
00:02:14,175 --> 00:02:16,655
[Kai, already pulling on his coat.]

78
00:02:14,275 --> 00:02:16,435
NARRATOR: Everyone's going to the shops.

79
00:02:16,805 --> 00:02:19,365
[Ito, already holding out a list.]

80
00:02:16,905 --> 00:02:19,145
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

81
00:02:19,515 --> 00:02:22,665
[Day 1. The whole street runs the same way: toward the supermarket.]

82
00:02:19,615 --> 00:02:23,080
NARRATOR: Kai was right. The whole city had the same idea.

83
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:26,285
[Half-empty shelves. Kai steers a trolley, reading Ito’s list.]

84
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:26,065
NARRATOR: Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

85
00:02:26,435 --> 00:02:28,285
[Mira marches back with an armful of dog treats. Biscuit loses his mind.]

86
00:02:26,535 --> 00:02:27,975
MIRA: They're on my list.

87
00:02:28,435 --> 00:02:31,585
[Two grown men in a tug-of-war over the last electric heater.]

88
00:02:28,535 --> 00:02:31,375
NARRATOR: Two grown men fought over the last heater.

89
00:02:31,745 --> 00:02:34,895
[Ito watches the fight, deadpan.]

90
00:02:31,845 --> 00:02:35,310
MR. ITO: Heaters need electricity. They'll learn.

91
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:38,830
[Day 2. The radio on the table, the announcer reading out messages.]

92
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:39,845
(RADIO): Now, messages from hospital staff to their families.

93
00:02:40,415 --> 00:02:43,565
[Then a tired woman’s voice.]

94
00:02:40,515 --> 00:02:45,455
NARRATOR: Kai, Mira, it's Mum. They need me at the hospital. Stay with Mr. Ito.

95
00:02:45,825 --> 00:02:48,975
[Kai and Mira on the floor of the living room, close together, listening.]

96
00:02:45,925 --> 00:02:49,010
NARRATOR: Mum was a nurse. She'd been on the night shift.

97
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:51,765
[Kai, finally asking it.]

98
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:51,245
NARRATOR: Is the Sun… dead?

99
00:02:51,915 --> 00:02:55,065
[Diagram: the Moon tugs a bulge of ocean toward it. The hidden Sun tugs too.]

100
00:02:52,015 --> 00:02:55,555
MR. ITO: The Moon pulls the sea. So does the Sun.

101
00:02:55,925 --> 00:02:59,075
[The Sun’s bulge is removed: the tide shrinks by a third.]

102
00:02:56,025 --> 00:03:00,540
MR. ITO: If the Sun were gone, today's tide would be a third smaller.

103
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:02,895
[Ito hands Kai an old tide table.]

104
00:03:01,010 --> 00:03:02,675
MR. ITO: Go and look.

105
00:03:03,045 --> 00:03:06,055
[The harbour. Torchlight on boats and black water.]

106
00:03:03,145 --> 00:03:05,835
NARRATOR: So, Kai and I walked down to the harbour.

107
00:03:06,205 --> 00:03:08,485
[The torch finds the painted tide gauge. The water sits exactly on Ito’s pencilled mark.]

108
00:03:06,305 --> 00:03:08,265
MIRA: It's exactly on the line!

109
00:03:08,635 --> 00:03:10,915
[Kai lowers the tide table.]

110
00:03:08,735 --> 00:03:10,695
NARRATOR: So the Sun's still there.

111
00:03:11,065 --> 00:03:13,095
[Mira looks up at the black sky.]

112
00:03:11,165 --> 00:03:12,575
MIRA: Then where is it?

113
00:03:13,245 --> 00:03:16,395
[The two of them, tiny at the edge of the frozen harbour, staring at where the Sun should be.]

114
00:03:13,345 --> 00:03:17,205
NARRATOR: The Sun hadn't gone anywhere. Something was in front of it.

115
00:03:18,075 --> 00:03:21,225
[Mira’s mitten points down through a thin skin of new ice: under it, a fish glides past in the torch beam.]

116
00:03:18,175 --> 00:03:21,340
MIRA: The sea's growing a skin. The fish don't mind!

117
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:24,860
[Day 3. Back on the roof in coats. Breath smoking.]

118
00:03:21,810 --> 00:03:25,195
NARRATOR: On Day 3, the sky did something beautiful.

119
00:03:25,565 --> 00:03:28,715
[The whole sky ignites: green and violet curtains over the frozen city.]

120
00:03:25,665 --> 00:03:30,980
(RADIO): …a solar storm that left the Sun four days ago, still arriving tonight.

121
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,935
[Mira, face lit green, watching.]

122
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:33,115
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

123
00:03:34,085 --> 00:03:36,935
[The young astronomer pins three plates to the wall beside a star chart.]

124
00:03:37,085 --> 00:03:40,235
[Red lines, extended from each plate, meet at one faint star on the chart. He writes 22 beside it.]

125
00:03:40,685 --> 00:03:43,715
[The city from above. Districts go dark one after another, rolling toward their street.]

126
00:03:40,785 --> 00:03:43,495
NARRATOR: On Day 7, the power went out.

127
00:03:43,865 --> 00:03:47,015
[Kai turns the bath tap on full while Ito watches from the door.]

128
00:03:43,965 --> 00:03:47,280
MR. ITO: Fill the bath. Before the pipes freeze.

129
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:50,800
[Blankets taped over the windows of one room. Mira hands up the tape.]

130
00:03:47,750 --> 00:03:51,160
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito showed us how to make one warm room.

131
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:54,435
[Kai carries the food from the dead freezer out onto the frosted balcony.]

132
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:54,215
NARRATOR: Free fridge. Finally, some good news.

133
00:03:54,585 --> 00:03:57,735
[Kai strikes a match and lights the candles.]

134
00:03:54,685 --> 00:03:57,670
NARRATOR: That night, Kai pretended he wasn't scared.

135
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:01,190
[Close in candlelight: Kai’s hand clamped round Mira’s mitten, knuckles pale.]

136
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,555
NARRATOR: He was holding my hand too hard.

137
00:04:01,425 --> 00:04:03,485
[Torch beam in the stairwell. Grey smoke curls out from under a neighbour’s door.]

138
00:04:01,525 --> 00:04:03,265
NARRATOR: Then we smelled smoke.

139
00:04:03,635 --> 00:04:06,785
[The torch finds door 3B: firelight flickering underneath, smoke pouring out.]

140
00:04:03,735 --> 00:04:06,950
MR. ITO: They're burning furniture. Indoors.

141
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:10,470
[Ito, in the smoke, suddenly very loud.]

142
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:11,860
MR. ITO: Everyone out. The smoke you can see. The gas you can't.

143
00:04:12,230 --> 00:04:15,135
[Down the stairs: neighbours coughing into their sleeves, Kai with the torch, Mira at his heels, Ito with his cane, Biscuit ahead.]

144
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:14,915
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

145
00:04:15,285 --> 00:04:18,165
[The street door bangs open into a wall of wind and snow. Biscuit bolts into the dark.]

146
00:04:15,385 --> 00:04:17,945
NARRATOR: At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

147
00:04:18,315 --> 00:04:20,770
[Mira screams his name into the storm. Nothing comes back.]

148
00:04:18,415 --> 00:04:19,550
MIRA: BISCUIT!

149
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:22,905
[Day 8. Kai walks the frozen street alone with a torch. Buried cars. Silent snow.]

150
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:22,685
NARRATOR: Biscuit! Come on, boy!

151
00:04:23,055 --> 00:04:26,205
[Kai turns on the spot, sweeping the torch. Nothing.]

152
00:04:23,155 --> 00:04:27,295
NARRATOR: Kai searched all night. Then again, it was always night now.

153
00:04:27,665 --> 00:04:29,715
[Somewhere far off, a single bark (a little "WOOF!" pops out of the dark). Kai’s head snaps round.]

154
00:04:29,865 --> 00:04:32,270
[The torch beam finds a line of fresh paw prints in the snow.]

155
00:04:29,965 --> 00:04:32,050
NARRATOR: Paw prints. Fresh ones.

156
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:35,570
[The prints lead across the street to a cloud of steam rising from a metro entrance.]

157
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:35,505
NARRATOR: They led to the only warm thing on the street.

158
00:04:35,875 --> 00:04:38,360
[Biscuit is curled against the warm grate, frost on his whiskers. He sees Kai. His tail explodes.]

159
00:04:35,975 --> 00:04:38,140
NARRATOR: You stupid, brilliant dog.

160
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:41,660
[Cut-away of the ground: frozen surface, then the metro tunnels, then warm rock below.]

161
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:42,670
NARRATOR: A few metres down, the ground stays around ten degrees.

162
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:46,190
[Biscuit trots toward the stairs and looks back. Come on.]

163
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:46,700
NARRATOR: Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd found the way down.

164
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:50,220
[Down the metro stairs: Kai carrying Mira on his back, Ito, and behind them a line of torches.]

165
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:50,755
NARRATOR: By morning, half the street had followed him underground.

166
00:04:51,125 --> 00:04:54,275
[Day 30. The metro is a town: tents on the platforms, string lights, laundry lines.]

167
00:04:51,225 --> 00:04:55,010
NARRATOR: By Day 30, Harbour Street station was a town.

168
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:58,530
[A disused tunnel full of grow lights and lettuce. A chalk menu: lettuce, lettuce, lettuce. A small kid glares at yet another lettuce.]

169
00:04:55,480 --> 00:05:00,215
NARRATOR: We grew food under pink lamps. Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

170
00:05:00,585 --> 00:05:03,735
[Seven a.m. The big lamps in the station roof warm up from orange to gold.]

171
00:05:00,685 --> 00:05:05,070
NARRATOR: At seven every morning, the engineers turned on the big lamps.

172
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:07,900
[Faces turned up into the gold light. Mira jumps. Ito smiles.]

173
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:07,680
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

174
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:11,200
[In a parked carriage turned classroom, Mira (the tallest child in the room) teaches four much smaller kids to draw a sun.]

175
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:11,810
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And it's warm on your face.

176
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:15,330
[A small kid holds up her drawing: a grey sun.]

177
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:15,565
NARRATOR: Some of them had already forgotten what colour it was.

178
00:05:16,535 --> 00:05:19,685
[Biscuit, noticeably rounder, receives snacks from a ring of adoring kids.]

179
00:05:16,635 --> 00:05:19,800
NARRATOR: Biscuit got a fan club. And a bigger belly.

180
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:23,320
[A typed letter under the lamp: the Council cannot support further observations, nor any transmission. His thumb covers the name.]

181
00:05:23,570 --> 00:05:26,620
[The receiver at his ear. Down the line, a colleague laughs. He says nothing.]

182
00:05:26,770 --> 00:05:29,920
[Ito at a workbench, a firefighter’s thermal camera in pieces under a magnifier light.]

183
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:31,235
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito spent weeks rebuilding a firefighter's camera.

184
00:05:31,605 --> 00:05:34,755
[He looks up at Kai over his glasses.]

185
00:05:31,705 --> 00:05:37,145
MR. ITO: It sees heat, not light. If something's out there, it has to be warm.

186
00:05:37,515 --> 00:05:40,665
[Day 40. They climb out into a dead white city.]

187
00:05:37,615 --> 00:05:40,850
NARRATOR: On Day 40, he took us up to the surface.

188
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:44,175
[He raises the camera at an empty patch of black sky.]

189
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,955
NARRATOR: He pointed it where the Sun should have been.

190
00:05:44,325 --> 00:05:46,660
[The camera screen, false colour. Black sky, and in the middle, a faint warm disc.]

191
00:05:44,425 --> 00:05:45,940
MR. ITO: It's warm.

192
00:05:46,810 --> 00:05:49,960
[Ito lowers the camera. His glasses have frosted.]

193
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:50,300
MR. ITO: And it's twice as wide as the Sun.

194
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:53,125
[Pull back from where the Sun should be: a colossal sphere of dark slats wrapped around the star, faintly glowing at the seams.]

195
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:52,905
NARRATOR: There was something around the Sun.

196
00:05:53,275 --> 00:05:56,425
[The camera glides around the shell. Each of its slats is wider than Earth.]

197
00:05:53,375 --> 00:05:55,690
NARRATOR: A shell. Wrapped around a star.

198
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,865
[Back below, in the workshop. Mira looks up at Ito.]

199
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:58,245
MIRA: Who built it?

200
00:05:59,015 --> 00:06:01,845
[Kai, shaking his head.]

201
00:05:59,115 --> 00:06:01,625
NARRATOR: Nobody could build that in a day.

202
00:06:01,995 --> 00:06:04,730
[Ito reaches into the canvas bag.]

203
00:06:02,095 --> 00:06:04,210
MR. ITO: No. Nobody did.

204
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:08,030
[An old eclipse photograph in Ito’s hand: three faint, perfectly straight lines crossing the corona.]

205
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:08,345
MR. ITO: Nineteen-nineteen. Look at the lines.

206
00:06:08,715 --> 00:06:11,865
[A window blind with its slats open, sunlight streaming through. Beside it, the Sun in a ring of the same slats, edge-on, light pouring between them.]

207
00:06:08,815 --> 00:06:13,380
MR. ITO: It was always there. Edge-on, like the slats of a blind.

208
00:06:13,750 --> 00:06:16,900
[Every slat turns at once. The window goes dark. The Sun’s light is trapped inside.]

209
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:17,990
MR. ITO: And on the morning the Sun went out, someone closed it.

210
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:21,170
[Mira, sharp.]

211
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:20,950
MIRA: You've seen those lines before?

212
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,470
[Ito, not meeting her eyes.]

213
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:26,135
MR. ITO: I saw them once. A long time ago. Nobody listened.

214
00:06:26,905 --> 00:06:30,055
[The two of them in the lamplight. Mira watches him.]

215
00:06:27,005 --> 00:06:29,645
NARRATOR: I believed him. It was even true.

216
00:06:30,615 --> 00:06:33,765
[Day 100. The lamps flicker, buzz, and die. Darkness, then a hundred torches clicking on.]

217
00:06:30,715 --> 00:06:34,080
NARRATOR: On Day 100, the generators failed.

218
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:37,600
[Ito follows the engineers into the dark tunnel, torch in hand.]

219
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:37,915
MR. ITO: I know a little about old machines.

220
00:06:38,285 --> 00:06:40,565
[Under every blanket they own, Kai and Mira shiver. Breath smoking even down here.]

221
00:06:38,385 --> 00:06:40,345
NARRATOR: That was the coldest night.

222
00:06:40,715 --> 00:06:43,865
[Kai’s face in the last candle light. For once, no joke.]

223
00:06:40,815 --> 00:06:42,880
NARRATOR: How does this end, Mira?

224
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:46,985
[Mira, small and certain.]

225
00:06:44,150 --> 00:06:46,165
MIRA: With the lights coming back on.

226
00:06:47,135 --> 00:06:49,970
[Day 180. The lamps blaze back on. The whole station roars.]

227
00:06:47,235 --> 00:06:49,750
NARRATOR: On Day 180, they did.

228
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:52,855
[A woman in a navy coat over nurse’s scrubs walks out of the tunnel. Mira and Kai run.]

229
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:51,835
NARRATOR: And so did Mum.

230
00:06:53,005 --> 00:06:55,215
[Mum turns to Ito, grease-stained and grinning.]

231
00:06:53,105 --> 00:06:54,995
NARRATOR: You kept them alive.

232
00:06:55,365 --> 00:06:58,075
[Ito, quietly.]

233
00:06:55,465 --> 00:06:57,455
MR. ITO: They kept me alive.

234
00:06:58,225 --> 00:07:00,075
[Later. Ito at his bench with wires and a little dish. Mira leans in.]

235
00:06:58,325 --> 00:06:59,785
MIRA: What are you building?

236
00:07:00,225 --> 00:07:03,160
[Ito, holding it up.]

237
00:07:00,325 --> 00:07:02,540
MR. ITO: Something to listen with.

238
00:07:03,310 --> 00:07:06,460
[Day 300. The surface again. Ito’s camera has a much bigger lens.]

239
00:07:03,410 --> 00:07:07,595
NARRATOR: By Day 300, his camera had a much bigger lens.

240
00:07:07,965 --> 00:07:11,115
[Thermal view: the warm disc, and from it a thin glowing thread running off into space.]

241
00:07:08,065 --> 00:07:10,925
NARRATOR: A thin, warm thread was leaving the shell.

242
00:07:11,295 --> 00:07:14,445
[The solar system tilts to the side: the beam rises straight out of the plane the planets move in.]

243
00:07:11,395 --> 00:07:15,860
MR. ITO: It's sending the light away. Out of the plane of the planets.

244
00:07:16,230 --> 00:07:18,080
[Kai and Mira, watching Ito.]

245
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:17,845
NARRATOR: Sending it where?

246
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:20,680
[The camera follows the thread to its end: one faint star, ringed on the screen.]

247
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:23,790
[Ito goes white. Barely a whisper.]

248
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:22,570
MR. ITO: Twenty-two.

249
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:27,090
[They stand in the snow. Mira watches Ito. He says nothing more.]

250
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:28,180
NARRATOR: He whispered a number. Twenty-two. I didn't ask what it meant.

251
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:31,700
[The shell from far away, a needle of energy leaving it toward one faint star.]

252
00:07:28,650 --> 00:07:31,490
NARRATOR: All the Sun's light, sent to one star.

253
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:35,110
[At the transmitter. His finger hovers over the key. He looks at the chart: 22.]

254
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:36,710
[He presses it, once.]

255
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:40,010
[Day 365. All of them on the surface, tiny under the brightest Milky Way anyone has ever seen.]

256
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:41,875
NARRATOR: On Day 365, we all went up to the surface together.

257
00:07:42,245 --> 00:07:45,330
[Ito sets up his little receiver dish on the snow.]

258
00:07:42,345 --> 00:07:45,110
MR. ITO: I need to point this at the Sun.

259
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:48,630
[Mira grins up at Kai.]

260
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:49,094
MIRA: One whole lap around the Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

261
00:07:49,464 --> 00:07:51,999
[The receiver clicks. Ito’s face changes.]

262
00:07:49,564 --> 00:07:51,779
MR. ITO: It's coming from the Sun.

263
00:07:52,149 --> 00:07:55,299
[Pulses: one, then two, then three. Kai counts along.]

264
00:07:52,249 --> 00:07:53,034
NARRATOR: One.

265
00:07:53,784 --> 00:07:54,319
NARRATOR: Two.

266
00:07:55,069 --> 00:07:55,804
NARRATOR: Three.

267
00:07:56,874 --> 00:07:59,259
[The planets in a row. Mercury lights up. Venus. Earth.]

268
00:07:56,974 --> 00:07:59,039
NARRATOR: Mercury. Venus. Earth.

269
00:07:59,409 --> 00:08:02,269
[The count stops on Earth. Earth, alone, highlighted.]

270
00:07:59,509 --> 00:08:01,249
NARRATOR: It stopped at three.

271
00:08:02,419 --> 00:08:04,947
[Mira, very quietly.]

272
00:08:02,519 --> 00:08:03,727
MIRA: That's us.

273
00:08:05,097 --> 00:08:08,247
[Ito shakes his head, slowly.]

274
00:08:05,197 --> 00:08:06,162
MR. ITO: No.

275
00:08:07,112 --> 00:08:09,652
MR. ITO: It isn't counting planets.

276
00:08:10,622 --> 00:08:13,772
[The Morse key again. One… two-two… three-three-three: the same rhythm as the pulses.]

277
00:08:14,222 --> 00:08:17,372
[He walks out of the dome. Over the far ridge, the sky goes gold, and the Sun rises.]

278
00:08:18,822 --> 00:08:20,872
[Close: his hand on the dome railing in the sunrise. Tap. Tap. Tap.]

279
00:08:21,022 --> 00:08:23,872
[His face in the sunrise, round glasses glinting.]

280
00:08:24,022 --> 00:08:27,172
[Match cut: the same face, forty-four years older, in starlight, tapping his cane three times.]

281
00:08:24,122 --> 00:08:29,037
NARRATOR: The man in the observatory was Mr. Ito. Forty-four years ago.

282
00:08:29,807 --> 00:08:32,957
[The star chart again: the three lines meeting at one star. 22.]

283
00:08:29,907 --> 00:08:33,122
NARRATOR: He found the lines, and the star they pointed to.

284
00:08:33,492 --> 00:08:36,642
[His young hand on the key.]

285
00:08:33,592 --> 00:08:36,182
NARRATOR: And he knocked. One. Two. Three.

286
00:08:36,952 --> 00:08:40,102
[Earth and a star 22 light-years apart. The knock crosses out: 1982 → 2004. The answer crosses back: 2004 → 2026.]

287
00:08:37,052 --> 00:08:41,167
NARRATOR: Twenty-two years to get there. Twenty-two years to come back.

288
00:08:41,537 --> 00:08:44,687
[Ito turns to Mira.]

289
00:08:41,637 --> 00:08:45,852
MR. ITO: It's been knocking back since the morning the Sun went out.

290
00:08:46,222 --> 00:08:49,372
[Close on Ito.]

291
00:08:46,322 --> 00:08:50,037
MR. ITO: I was the only one who knew where to listen.

292
00:08:50,407 --> 00:08:53,557
[Ito, looking up at the black sky.]

293
00:08:50,507 --> 00:08:52,547
MR. ITO: It isn't counting.

294
00:08:53,197 --> 00:08:54,962
MR. ITO: It's answering.

295
00:08:56,132 --> 00:08:59,282
[Ito looks at Mira. Mira steps in and takes his hand.]

296
00:08:56,232 --> 00:08:58,397
MR. ITO: I'm sorry, Mira.

297
00:08:59,967 --> 00:09:03,117
[Sydney, Day 365: the sea frozen mid-wave, snow on the sand. The surfer in a parka, a radio pulsing in her hands.]

298
00:09:00,067 --> 00:09:02,957
NARRATOR: That night, Mr. Ito told the world.

299
00:09:03,327 --> 00:09:05,907
[The farmer in his snow-buried field, listening.]

300
00:09:03,427 --> 00:09:05,687
NARRATOR: Every radio on Earth heard it.

301
00:09:06,057 --> 00:09:07,907
[The boy at the snowed-in bus stop, a radio pulsing in his hands.]

302
00:09:08,057 --> 00:09:11,207
[A memory, warm as home movie: the same roof, every dawn for forty-four years. Mr. Ito, young, then greying, then old, taps his telescope three times as the Sun comes up.]

303
00:09:08,157 --> 00:09:12,992
NARRATOR: Every morning for forty-four years, Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

304
00:09:13,962 --> 00:09:17,112
[Close on his face in the early light.]

305
00:09:14,062 --> 00:09:17,097
NARRATOR: I used to think it was because he liked it.

306
00:09:18,867 --> 00:09:22,017
[The end screen: the shell turning, its three pulses, THE REPLY, and room for the subscribe and next-video cards.]

307
00:09:18,967 --> 00:09:21,407
NARRATOR: Whoever answered is still out there.

308
00:09:21,757 --> 00:09:24,742
NARRATOR: What would you say back? Tell us in the comments.
